  <h1>How FPGAs Are Used in HFT</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: the <code>fpga/</code> folder
    holds three working VHDL modules — <code>market_maker.vhd</code>,
    <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code>, and
    <code>moving_average_trader.vhd</code>. To extend them honestly you need to
    understand <em>what</em> FPGAs do in HFT systems and, just as important,
    <em>what they don't do</em>.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 WHY FPGAS ===================== -->
  <h2>1. Why an FPGA, and not a fast CPU?</h2>

  <p>
    A CPU is a general-purpose machine. It fetches instructions, predicts branches,
    fights cache misses, and obeys an operating system that can interrupt it at any
    moment. None of that is free, and the variability — the <strong>jitter</strong> —
    is what kills HFT strategies, even more than the average latency.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <strong>FPGA (Field-Programmable Gate Array)</strong> is a sea of configurable
    logic blocks (LUTs), flip-flops, DSP slices, and routing fabric. You describe
    your trading logic in HDL (VHDL or Verilog), the tools <em>synthesize</em> that
    description into a wire-level circuit, and the chip becomes that circuit until
    you reload it. There is no instruction stream. There is no OS. There is a clock,
    and on every rising edge, every register in your design updates simultaneously.
  </p>

  <h3>The numbers that justify the complexity</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Path</th><th>Tick-to-trade</th><th>Jitter</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Python over the internet</td>
      <td>~50–500 ms</td>
      <td>Massive (GIL, network)</td>
      <td>Where <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> lives.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Tuned C++ with kernel bypass (DPDK / Solarflare OpenOnload)</td>
      <td>~750–800 ns</td>
      <td>Low (~100 ns)</td>
      <td>20–30% latency win vs vanilla TCP.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FPGA NIC (parsing in hardware → strategy on chip → packet out)</td>
      <td>~100–500 ns</td>
      <td>Near-zero (deterministic)</td>
      <td>Where <code>market_maker.vhd</code> wants to live.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    Sources: industry summaries on Solarflare OpenOnload latencies and FPGA tick-to-trade in 2025 (see chat).
  </p>

  <h3>What an FPGA actually buys you (4 things)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Determinism.</strong> No OS scheduler, no cache, no branch predictor. The same input takes the same number of cycles every single time. Strategies are easier to certify against exchange latency floors.</li>
    <li><strong>True parallelism.</strong> The 3-feature dot product in <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> computes <em>all three multiplies in the same cycle</em>. A CPU pipelines them serially.</li>
    <li><strong>Wire-speed I/O.</strong> The NIC <em>is</em> the FPGA. Market data packets enter the PHY and are parsed inside the same chip that emits the order packet — no PCIe round-trip, no kernel hop.</li>
    <li><strong>Low and predictable power.</strong> An Artix-7 fabric burns 5–25 W. A tuned Xeon burns hundreds. Power matters in colocation cabinets where thermal headroom is the real constraint.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>What FPGAs <em>don't</em> buy you:</strong> developer velocity, complex
    branching logic, or floating-point ML. Every change requires a 10-minute-to-hours
    synthesis run. Decision trees are notoriously bad on FPGAs (the
    <code>fpga/README.md</code> notes this on lines 152–155). Big neural nets need
    HLS toolchains like hls4ml or FINN — not handwritten VHDL.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 PIPELINE PATTERN ===================== -->
  <h2>2. The universal HFT-FPGA pattern: registered pipelines + valid handshakes</h2>

  <p>
    Almost every HFT FPGA module — including all three in this repo — follows the
    same shape:
  </p>

  <div class="pipeline">
    <div class="stage"><span class="num">0</span><span class="what">Market data tick arrives at the PHY (e.g. parsed from a 10 GbE UDP packet by an on-chip decoder)</span><span class="lat">~25 ns</span></div>
    <div class="arrow">▼</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="num">1</span><span class="what">Feature extraction stage — registered. <em>Combinational</em> arithmetic this cycle, latched at the next clock edge.</span><span class="lat">4 ns @ 250 MHz</span></div>
    <div class="arrow">▼</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="num">2</span><span class="what">Decision stage — registered. Threshold compare, signal generation, risk check.</span><span class="lat">4 ns</span></div>
    <div class="arrow">▼</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="num">3</span><span class="what">Order build + emit — registered. Pack a FIX/OUCH message, hand to MAC, out the SFP+.</span><span class="lat">~50 ns</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>Two ideas to internalize:</p>

  <h3>(a) Registered pipeline</h3>
  <p>
    Each stage does some arithmetic <em>between</em> clock edges and <em>latches the result</em> at the next edge.
    More stages = lower clock period (faster clock, more throughput) but more cycles of latency. It's a knob, not a free win.
  </p>

  <h3>(b) The <code>valid</code> handshake</h3>
  <p>
    Every stage carries a <code>valid_in</code> bit alongside its data. The next stage only
    accepts and processes data when <code>valid_in = '1'</code>. After processing, it asserts
    <code>valid_out = '1'</code> for exactly one cycle so the next consumer knows the output
    is real. This is the universal solution to "what does this cycle's register actually mean?"
  </p>

<pre><span class="com">-- The pattern, in any HFT VHDL module ever written:</span>
<span class="kw">process</span>(clk)
<span class="kw">begin</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> rising_edge(clk) <span class="kw">then</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> rst_n = <span class="str">'0'</span> <span class="kw">then</span>
            <span class="com">-- everything to zero on reset</span>
            valid_out <= <span class="str">'0'</span>;
        <span class="kw">else</span>
            valid_out <= valid_in;   <span class="com">-- carry the valid bit forward</span>
            <span class="kw">if</span> valid_in = <span class="str">'1'</span> <span class="kw">then</span>
                <span class="com">-- ONLY compute when the input is real</span>
                result_reg <= some_arithmetic(input_reg);
            <span class="kw">end if</span>;
        <span class="kw">end if</span>;
    <span class="kw">end if</span>;
<span class="kw">end process</span>;
</pre>

  <!-- ===================== §3 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>3. The three modules in <code>fpga/</code>, read as one system</h2>

  <p>
    Imagine the data flow that <em>doesn't exist yet</em> in the repo but is implied by the modules:
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    market data → <code>linear_regression_predictor</code> &nbsp;OR&nbsp; <code>moving_average_trader</code> → trading signal → <code>market_maker</code> → quotes out
  </p>

  <h3>(a) <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> — a 2-stage dot product</h3>
  <span class="filepath">fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd (lines 107–124, condensed)</span>
<pre><span class="com">-- Stage 1: three multiplies, all running in parallel on the SAME cycle.</span>
stage1_multiply : <span class="kw">process</span>(clk)
<span class="kw">begin</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> rising_edge(clk) <span class="kw">then</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> valid_in = <span class="str">'1'</span> <span class="kw">then</span>
            prod_f0 <= W_F0 * feature_0;   <span class="com">-- 0.30 × price</span>
            prod_f1 <= W_F1 * feature_1;   <span class="com">-- -0.10 × imbalance</span>
            prod_f2 <= W_F2 * feature_2;   <span class="com">-- 0.20 × volatility</span>
        <span class="kw">end if</span>;
    <span class="kw">end if</span>;
<span class="kw">end process</span>;
</pre>
  <p>
    Three DSP slices, fired simultaneously, all done in one 4 ns cycle.
    A CPU would dispatch those multiplies sequentially through its FPU.
  </p>

  <span class="filepath">fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd (lines 139–155, condensed)</span>
<pre><span class="com">-- Stage 2: sum the products, add the intercept, threshold to a 2-bit signal.</span>
acc := prod_f0 + prod_f1 + prod_f2 + resize(W0 * to_signed(<span class="num">1000</span>, <span class="num">32</span>), <span class="num">64</span>);

<span class="kw">if</span> acc &gt; THRESHOLD_POS <span class="kw">then</span>
    signal_r <= <span class="str">"01"</span>;   <span class="com">-- BUY</span>
<span class="kw">elsif</span> acc &lt; THRESHOLD_NEG <span class="kw">then</span>
    signal_r <= <span class="str">"10"</span>;   <span class="com">-- SELL</span>
<span class="kw">else</span>
    signal_r <= <span class="str">"00"</span>;   <span class="com">-- HOLD</span>
<span class="kw">end if</span>;
</pre>
  <p>
    Notice the integer scaling: weights are <code>×1000</code>, features are <code>×1000</code>,
    so the accumulator is at <code>×10⁶</code>. That's how the comparator can be a pure
    integer compare with no floating-point cost. <strong>Total module latency: 2 cycles ≈ 8 ns.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>(b) <code>moving_average_trader.vhd</code> — a O(1) sliding window</h3>
  <p>
    A naive moving average over a 20-sample window would need 20 adders in series.
    This module uses the classic incremental trick: <em>add the newest sample, subtract the oldest</em>.
    One adder, one subtractor, every cycle, regardless of window size.
  </p>
  <span class="filepath">fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd (lines 136–149, condensed)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">if</span> warmed_up = <span class="str">'1'</span> <span class="kw">then</span>
    <span class="com">-- Full window: subtract outgoing, add incoming. O(1) regardless of WINDOW.</span>
    running_sum <= running_sum
                   + resize(price_in,         SUM_BITS)
                   - resize(price_reg(WINDOW-<span class="num">1</span>), SUM_BITS);
<span class="kw">else</span>
    <span class="com">-- Warm-up: just accumulate the first WINDOW samples.</span>
    running_sum <= running_sum + resize(price_in, SUM_BITS);
<span class="kw">end if</span>;
</pre>
  <p>
    This is why HFT FPGA code looks so unfamiliar — every algorithm has to be re-expressed
    so it fits in a fixed-depth combinational chain that meets the clock period. A Python
    one-liner like <code>df['close'].rolling(20).mean()</code> hides hundreds of operations;
    on the FPGA each one of those operations is silicon you have to lay out.
  </p>

  <h3>(c) <code>market_maker.vhd</code> — quote generator (covered in Lesson 0001)</h3>
  <p>
    Reminder of how it fits: when the predictor emits a non-HOLD signal, the market maker
    block is what actually produces the <code>buy_price_out</code> and <code>sell_price_out</code>
    to send to the exchange. The predictor decides <em>whether</em> to quote; the market maker
    decides <em>what</em> the quote is. 1 clock-cycle latency (4 ns).
  </p>

  <h3>End-to-end latency budget for this project (theoretical)</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Latency</th><th>Status in repo</th></tr>
    <tr><td>10 GbE UDP parse</td><td>~25 ns</td><td><em>Not built</em> (README "Future Roadmap")</td></tr>
    <tr><td>linear_regression_predictor (2 stages)</td><td>~8 ns</td><td><strong>Built</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>market_maker (1 stage)</td><td>~4 ns</td><td><strong>Built</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Order encode + TX</td><td>~50 ns</td><td><em>Not built</em></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Total tick-to-trade (estimated)</th><th>~90 ns</th><th></th></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    Compare to the ~750–800 ns of a Solarflare-class CPU stack. The arithmetic kernels
    in this repo are already in the right ballpark for top-quartile HFT. The missing
    pieces are I/O, not math.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §4 OFTEN CONFUSED WITH ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Often confused with</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>FPGA vs ASIC</h4>
      <p>
        An <strong>ASIC</strong> is silicon manufactured for one specific design — fastest
        possible, but tape-out costs millions and the design is frozen forever.
        An <strong>FPGA</strong> is generic silicon you reprogram in seconds via a new
        bitstream. HFT firms iterate strategies weekly; nobody tapes out an ASIC for that.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>FPGA vs GPU</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>GPU</strong> is massively parallel but its parallelism is SIMD across
        thousands of threads on the same instruction — great for training neural nets,
        wrong shape for "one tick → one quote." Latency from PCIe + driver alone exceeds
        what an FPGA's <em>entire</em> tick-to-trade budget allows.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>VHDL vs Verilog</h4>
      <p>
        Two HDLs that compile to the same gates. <strong>VHDL</strong> is verbose,
        strongly typed, popular in defense/aerospace and this repo.
        <strong>Verilog</strong> (and SystemVerilog) is terser and more common at
        web-scale silicon firms. Religious choice; not technical.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>HDL vs HLS</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>HDL</strong> (VHDL/Verilog) describes hardware register-by-register.
        <strong>HLS</strong> (High-Level Synthesis) takes annotated C++ and generates
        HDL for you. Faster to write; sometimes worse area/timing. <code>hls_synthesis.tcl</code>
        in this repo drives the HLS flow on the hand-written VHDL.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Combinational vs Sequential logic</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Combinational</strong> logic produces outputs that depend only on
        current inputs — no clock involved. <strong>Sequential</strong> logic uses
        flip-flops driven by a clock, holding state between cycles. HFT pipelines are
        combinational <em>between</em> stages, sequential <em>at</em> each stage boundary.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Synthesis vs Place &amp; Route</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Synthesis</strong> turns VHDL into a netlist of generic gates.
        <strong>Place &amp; Route (PAR)</strong> assigns each gate to physical LUTs on
        the chip and routes the wires. Timing closure (meeting the clock period) is
        won or lost at PAR — that's why HFT engineers stare at PAR reports for hours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

