  <h2>5. Practice — click an answer for instant feedback</h2>
  <p class="small">Options shuffle on every page load. Read each one — distractors teach too.</p>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q1. <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> uses two pipeline stages. If you ran a single tick through it, how long until you got the trading signal at 250 MHz?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That would be one stage of 4 ns — but the module is explicitly a 2-stage pipeline (multiply, then accumulate + threshold).">~4 ns (one cycle).</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right — 2 stages × 4 ns/cycle = 8 ns from valid_in to valid_out for a single tick.">~8 ns (two cycles).</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — that's the period of a 10 GHz clock; the design runs at 250 MHz (4 ns per cycle).">~100 ps.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's roughly CPU-with-kernel-bypass tick-to-trade, not an FPGA stage of dot-product math.">~750 ns.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q2. Why does <code>moving_average_trader.vhd</code> maintain a <code>running_sum</code> and only add the new sample / subtract the oldest, instead of summing all 20 samples each cycle?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="VHDL syntax isn't the constraint — it's silicon. The question is what fits in one clock period.">Because VHDL can't express a 20-input adder.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct — an incremental update is one adder + one subtractor regardless of WINDOW, so it meets timing at 250 MHz. Summing 20 samples would need a 5-level adder tree that may not close.">Because the incremental update is O(1) and meets the clock-period budget regardless of window size; a 20-deep adder tree might fail timing.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The shift register and the running sum are independent — one stores samples, the other tracks the total.">Because there isn't enough BRAM for the shift register.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Floating point isn't involved — everything is integer-scaled ×100. The reason is timing, not numerics.">Because floating-point adders have too much rounding error.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q3. Your colleague wants to deploy a 100-tree <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> directly as hand-written VHDL on the FPGA. What's the best response?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Just throwing more LUTs at deep mux trees doesn't fix the fundamental shape mismatch with FPGA fabric — see the README note on this (lines 152–155).">"Sure, just use a bigger FPGA — Alveo U250 has plenty of LUTs."</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Trees and float comparisons make this expensive in hand-written HDL. The HLS / hls4ml route exists exactly because hand-coding doesn't scale.">"Yes, hand-written VHDL is always best for HFT."</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right — decision trees branch on float comparisons, producing deep multiplexer trees; 100 trees explodes LUT usage. The README points to hls4ml or Vitis AI as the practical path. Hand VHDL is for small dot products like the linear regressor.">"Hand-VHDL is great for the linear predictor, but 100-tree GBMs need an HLS toolchain like hls4ml or Vitis AI — the README documents exactly that."</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="A GPU's PCIe round-trip + driver latency already eats more than the entire FPGA tick-to-trade budget. Wrong shape entirely.">"Move it to a GPU instead — they're better at ML inference."</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q4. What does the <code>valid_in</code> / <code>valid_out</code> handshake in these modules actually do?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — reset is a separate signal (rst_n). valid_in says 'this cycle's data is real'; reset says 'wipe all state'.">It resets the module's internal state.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Exactly. Without a valid bit, a downstream block can't distinguish a fresh tick from stale register contents. The flag rides through every pipeline stage so each consumer knows whether to act.">It marks which cycles carry real data versus stale register contents, propagating through every pipeline stage so consumers know when to act.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="It is a single bit, not a checksum. The packet integrity check (if any) lives in the network layer, not here.">It's a checksum that verifies the input bits weren't corrupted.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — the clock is the clock. valid_in gates whether to use the data on a given clock edge, not the rate of the edges themselves.">It throttles the clock frequency of the module.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q5. Which statement about FPGA-NIC HFT systems is most accurate?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's the CPU-with-kernel-bypass path. FPGA NICs do parsing on-chip and skip PCIe entirely for the hot path.">Market data is parsed by the kernel, then DMA'd to the FPGA over PCIe for the strategy to run.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct — the FPGA IS the NIC. Packets enter the PHY and the parsing, strategy logic, and order emission all happen on the same chip. PCIe is only used for configuration and slow-path telemetry.">Packets enter the FPGA's own PHY, are parsed in-fabric, drive the strategy, and exit as orders on the same chip — PCIe is reserved for configuration and slow-path telemetry.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — top FPGA systems are at 100–500 ns tick-to-trade. Sub-nanosecond is below the speed of light across a typical board trace.">A modern FPGA NIC achieves sub-nanosecond tick-to-trade.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="FPGAs absolutely run an OS sometimes (Zynq has an ARM PS), but not in the hot path. The strategy logic lives in PL (programmable logic) with no OS in the way.">FPGAs run a tiny embedded Linux that processes ticks.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §6 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. You change the constant <code>W_F0</code> in <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> from <code>300</code> to <code>450</code>. What do you have to do before the change runs on the board?</summary>
    <p>
      You have to <strong>re-synthesize and re-place-and-route</strong> the design,
      then load the new bitstream onto the FPGA. The constants are baked into the
      configuration of the LUTs and DSP slices at synthesis time — there is no
      "runtime variable" to write to. This is why HFT firms that want
      online-tunable parameters wire them as <em>input ports</em> driven by a
      register file on the PCIe side, not as <code>constant</code>s.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. <code>moving_average_trader.vhd</code> has a <code>warmed_up</code> flag that only goes high after WINDOW samples have arrived. Why does <code>valid_s2</code> in stage 2 use <code>valid_s1 <strong>and</strong> warmed_s1</code>?</summary>
    <p>
      Because the moving average is meaningless during warm-up — you'd be dividing a
      partial sum by 20 instead of by the (smaller) number of samples actually seen.
      Emitting <code>valid_s2 = '0'</code> until warm-up is complete tells any
      downstream consumer "ignore my outputs; they're not real yet." This is the
      same valid-handshake discipline as in the rest of the file, just guarded by
      the additional warm-up condition.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. The README's roadmap mentions "10 GbE UDP stack" and "Risk engine in one clock cycle." Why are those the next two priorities if you wanted real tick-to-trade?</summary>
    <p>
      Because the three modules in <code>fpga/</code> only implement the
      <strong>arithmetic kernel</strong>. To call this end-to-end you need (a) a way
      to <em>receive</em> market data without going through the kernel, which is the
      10 GbE UDP stack, and (b) a <em>last-cycle gate</em> that vetoes orders that
      would breach position or notional limits — a hardware risk engine. Without (b)
      a single bug in the predictor can blow up the firm before any human can react.
      Without (a) you've built a Ferrari engine with no wheels.
    </p>
  </details>

